From 4eb1c4d43864bf815854083fa9e942df43ce9280 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] hwdec: H.264 on VideoCore behind XT_DOMA_HWDEC,
 SurfaceFlinger at 60 Hz

Let the guest decode H.264 on VideoCore. What it gets: the V4L2 Codec2
HAL (android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2) talking to /dev/video0,
which is a virtio-media device backed by bcm2835-codec in DomD, i.e.
VideoCore's own decoder.

The decode path is inside `ifeq ($(XT_DOMA_HWDEC),true)`, so a build
that does not pass the variable installs none of it. The service needs
a /dev/videoN, which DomA has only when its device model provides a
virtio-media device; declaring the HAL without one would leave init
crash-looping a service and the VINTF manifest advertising a HAL the
device does not have. Three things sit outside the gate: the soong
namespace line and the hal_codec2_default sepolicy, each deliberately,
and the SurfaceFlinger property described last, which applies to any
30 fps video.

  * PRODUCT_SOONG_NAMESPACES for external/v4l2_codec2. Its root
    Android.bp opens with a bare `soong_namespace {}`, which hides every
    module in the project from the build graph until a product opts in.
    A namespace opt-in installs nothing by itself, and keeping it
    unconditional means the gated and ungated builds share one soong
    analysis.
  * media_codecs_c2.xml describes DECODERS ONLY, AND H.264 ONLY.
    bcm2835-codec supports H.263, H.264, JPEG, MJPEG, MPEG2, MPEG4 and
    VC-1 -- no VP8, VP9, HEVC or AV1 -- so the VP8/VP9 entries in the
    upstream template would advertise codecs that cannot work. Encoders
    are dropped because encode is a different node (/dev/video11) and
    this device model proxies exactly one. The .secure variants are
    dropped because there is no Widevine and no secure allocator. max
    size is 1920x1088, not 4096x4096: VideoCore's H.264 decoder is a
    1080p part, and claiming 4K would let the framework hand it streams
    it cannot decode, which would look like a decoder bug.
  * codec2.vendor.ext.policy is the extended seccomp policy. It is
    installed as android.hardware.media.c2-extended-seccomp_policy, the
    fixed name the service opens; under any other name it is never read,
    and the V4L2 ioctls are killed rather than denied.
  * The gate also sets the properties the service and the framework
    read: the v4l2_codec2 instance counts, prefer_rgba, the
    capture-buffer output delay, the Codec2 pool mask and
    media.c2.hal.selection=hidl. The last one decides whether any of
    this is reachable: the service publishes a HIDL-only VINTF
    fragment, and with AIDL selected, which is what cuttlefish sets,
    the framework never enumerates this store and c2.v4l2.avc.decoder
    does not appear in the codec list. The software codecs stay, on the
    HIDL software instance. The makefile argues each property.
  * sepolicy for hal_codec2_default: the domain and exec type init needs
    before it can start the service at all -- file_contexts labels the
    service binary hal_codec2_default_exec, without which init cannot
    exec it -- plus rules for the denials collected from board logs,
    2026-09-01. The board boots permissive, so those denials break
    nothing today and would break hardware decode the moment it goes
    enforcing. It is unconditional so that every build type-checks the
    same policy.

ro.surface_flinger.enable_frame_rate_override=false keeps SurfaceFlinger
at 60 Hz while a 30 fps video plays. A 1080p30 clip through VideoView
swung between 14.7 and 28.4 fps from one 10 s window to the next. In
the bad windows SurfaceFlinger had dropped its render rate to 30 Hz for
the video (frame rate override with content detection): the work
duration went to 32.3 ms, the present time it handed drm_hwcomposer was
two vsyncs out, and WaitForPresentTime slept inside
PresentStagedComposition -- SurfaceFlinger's main thread -- 13 ms
median per frame. NuPlayer places frames on the 60 Hz grid, so when the
30 Hz grid's phase disagreed every frame was one vsync late and pairs
collapsed into one (4-vsync gaps for 52 of 73 frames, 54 buffers
released unshown).

The property keeps the render rate at 60 Hz. It also turns off per-app
frame rate overrides (setFrameRate, GameManager), which only ever lower
the render rate below the panel's; the guest's virtio-gpu offers one
60 Hz mode. Only the RPi4 device sets it; the Pi 5 has not been
measured. Measured at runtime on the RPi4 (setprop before
SurfaceFlinger starts, 2026-09-24): four runs at 27.1-28.8 fps, the
render rate stayed 60 Hz, and WaitForPresentTime never slept.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [automotive-grade-linux/Android_device_sodev_xenvm-cf]
---
 aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk                | 147 ++++++++++++++++++
 sepolicy/file_contexts                        |  14 ++
 sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te                |  89 +++++++++++
 .../codec2.vendor.ext.policy                  |  19 +++
 xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml    |  77 +++++++++
 5 files changed, 346 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te
 create mode 100644 xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/codec2.vendor.ext.policy
 create mode 100644 xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml

diff --git a/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk b/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
index 655b980..46fc236 100644
--- a/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
+++ b/aosp_xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64.mk
@@ -165,6 +165,23 @@ PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
     debug.renderengine.backend=skiavkthreaded \
     debug.hwui.renderer=skiavk
 
+# Keep SurfaceFlinger rendering at the panel's 60 Hz. With frame rate override
+# on (the default, SurfaceFlinger.cpp:4256), a lone 30 fps video makes
+# SurfaceFlinger drop its render rate to 30 Hz: its work duration becomes
+# 32.3 ms and it hands drm_hwcomposer a present time two vsyncs out, which
+# WaitForPresentTime (HwcDisplay.cpp:955-1001) sleeps off inside
+# PresentStagedComposition -- on SurfaceFlinger's main thread, 13 ms median per
+# frame. NuPlayer places frames on the 60 Hz grid, so whenever the 30 Hz grid's
+# phase disagrees every frame lands one vsync late and pairs collapse into one:
+# 10 s windows of the same 1080p30 clip measured 14.7 and 28.4 fps. With it off,
+# four runs gave 27.1-28.8 fps and WaitForPresentTime never slept. The same
+# property also defaults content detection off (SurfaceFlinger.cpp:5172), which
+# is what picked the 30 Hz rate. What is given up: per-app frame rate overrides
+# (setFrameRate, GameManager), i.e. lowering the render rate below the panel's;
+# virtio-gpu offers this guest a single 60 Hz mode anyway.
+PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
+    ro.surface_flinger.enable_frame_rate_override=false
+
 # Start the interface rename that minradio's data call depends on. cuttlefish already
 # ships the service and a trigger for it, but trout sets ro.vendor.disable_rename_eth0
 # so that trigger never fires; overriding that is deliberate and argued in the .rc.
@@ -336,6 +353,19 @@ PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
     bluetooth.sco.managed_by_audio=true \
     bluetooth.hfp.software_datapath.enabled=true
 
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY PRODUCT_SOONG_NAMESPACES for external/v4l2_codec2. Its root Android.bp
+# opens with a bare `soong_namespace {}`, which hides every module in the
+# project from the build graph until a product opts in. Without this line
+# `m libv4l2_codec2_common ...` fails with `unknown target` for all six
+# modules -- that is namespace invisibility, NOT an android17 source
+# incompatibility. external/v4l2_codec2/README.md names this exact line as
+# step 1 of the official integration recipe.
+#
+# Declaring a namespace only makes modules visible; it installs nothing, so this
+# line alone leaves the image byte-for-byte unaffected.
+PRODUCT_SOONG_NAMESPACES += external/v4l2_codec2
+
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # WHY DomaWifiRelay. It is the ONLY component that can learn the passphrase the user
 # types on the AAOS Wi-Fi screen. The shadow AP on wlan2 validates that passphrase
@@ -374,6 +404,123 @@ PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
     DomaWifiRelay
 
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# DomA HARDWARE DECODE, BEHIND A BUILD SWITCH.
+#
+# Everything in this block is gated on XT_DOMA_HWDEC=true, which the SoDeV
+# workspace's rpi4-sodev.yaml sets on its vulkan lane. The C2 service ships a
+# VINTF fragment declaring android.hardware.media.c2@1.2, and declaring a HAL
+# that cannot start (there is no /dev/videoN in DomA without the virtio-media
+# device, which only the qemu 10.0.2 device model has) would leave init
+# crash-looping a service and the VINTF manifest advertising a HAL the device
+# does not have.
+ifeq ($(XT_DOMA_HWDEC),true)
+
+# The V4L2 codec2 service and the plugin store it loads.
+PRODUCT_PACKAGES += \
+    android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2 \
+    libc2plugin_store
+
+# The extended seccomp policy MUST be installed under the name
+# android.hardware.media.c2-extended-seccomp_policy (confirmed on RPi4 hardware,
+# 2026-09-01): external/v4l2_codec2's service/service.cpp opens that fixed path
+# (kExtSeccompPolicyPath, under /vendor/etc/seccomp_policy/), so under the name
+# codec2.vendor.ext.policy the extended policy is never read and the V4L2 ioctls
+# are killed by seccomp. No SELinux denial is emitted, so the cause is
+# invisible.
+# Codec2InfoBuilder reads media_codecs_c2.xml to describe vendor C2 components.
+# Decoders only, H.264 only, 1080p max: the reasoning is in the file itself.
+PRODUCT_COPY_FILES += \
+    device/sodev/xenvm-cf/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml:$(TARGET_COPY_OUT_VENDOR)/etc/media_codecs_c2.xml \
+    device/sodev/xenvm-cf/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/codec2.vendor.ext.policy:$(TARGET_COPY_OUT_VENDOR)/etc/seccomp_policy/android.hardware.media.c2-extended-seccomp_policy
+
+# WHY prefer_rgba=true is spelled out here although it is v4l2_codec2's default.
+#
+# Both places that read it, DecodeInterface (the pixel format the component
+# advertises) and V4L2Decoder's preferRgbOutput() (the format it asks the
+# device for), take ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.prefer_rgba with true as the default,
+# so this line changes nothing the decoder does; it names the choice where the
+# product is defined. It is load bearing for this stack, not a preference: the
+# guest's GL driver has no GL_EXT_YUV_target, so a YUV capture buffer cannot
+# become a texture -- see the kPreferredRgbFourccs list in V4L2Decoder.cpp and
+# the paragraphs above it.
+PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
+    ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.decode_concurrent_instances=8 \
+    ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.encode_concurrent_instances=0 \
+    ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.prefer_rgba=true
+
+# CAPTURE buffer budget for 1080p on this board.
+#
+# external/v4l2_codec2's getMinNumOutputBuffers() is output_delay + 4 + 3 + 2 and
+# the H.264 default delay is 16, so a 1080p decode asks the host for 25 NV12
+# capture buffers of 3,133,440 B each (~75 MiB of order-10 CMA in DomD). DomD's
+# CMA is the only VideoCore-reachable pool and ~100 MiB of it is already held by
+# the vc4/virtio-gpu scanout buffers at idle, so the 24th/25th REQBUFS allocation
+# fails ("bcm2835-codec: dma alloc of size 3133440 failed", cma_alloc -EBUSY,
+# order-10 GFP_DMA failure) and the reclaim/compaction storm that follows starves
+# every DomD process for minutes (measured 2026-09-15). 854x480 works because
+# 25 x 691,200 B fits.
+#
+# Delay 2 = 11 buffers (~33 MiB) was the measured minimum that decodes 1080p30;
+# 720p passes with the code default. The code default stays 16 on purpose
+# (DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay); the property is read once per component
+# instance. Streams that reorder deeper than 2 frames make CCodec time out
+# instead of stalling -- raise to 4 if that shows up (the H.264 streams measured
+# in a WebView reordered by 2-4 frames). It pairs with DomD's CMA (cma= in
+# DomD's bootargs, boot.scr), which gives the pool the headroom the 2026-09-07
+# measurement (~45 MiB free) assumed.
+PRODUCT_PROPERTY_OVERRIDES += \
+    debug.v4l2codec2.output_delay=2
+
+# WHY media.c2.hal.selection=hidl, AND WHY WITHOUT IT NOTHING ABOVE IS REACHABLE.
+# external/v4l2_codec2's service publishes a HIDL-only VINTF fragment:
+#
+#     <hal format="hidl"><name>android.hardware.media.c2</name>
+#         <fqname>@1.2::IComponentStore/default</fqname>
+#
+# and the framework decides which transport to enumerate before it looks at any
+# manifest. IsCodec2AidlHalSelected() in
+# frameworks/av/media/codec2/hal/common/HalSelection.cpp picks AIDL only where
+# the aidl_hal aconfig flag is enabled (it IS, state: ENABLED in
+# build/release/aconfig/trunk_staging/com.android.media.codec.flags) or the
+# vendor API level is 202404 or later, and then only if media.c2.hal.selection
+# is aidl; the property itself defaults to hidl. cuttlefish sets it to aidl
+# (device/google/cuttlefish/shared/device.mk, with ?=), so the inherited value
+# was aidl, and with AIDL selected Codec2Client::CacheServiceNames()
+# (hal/client/client.cpp:2755) walks AServiceManager_forEachDeclaredInstance()
+# over AIDL instances only and never reaches the HIDL branch.
+#
+# MEASURED CONSEQUENCE: c2.v4l2.avc.decoder did not appear in the codec list even
+# with the virtio-media device, because Codec2InfoBuilder builds its list from the
+# traits the client returns and the client had never seen this store. The image
+# contained every hardware-decode artifact and used none of them.
+#
+# hidl rather than converting the service to AIDL because the device is
+# target-level 7 and compatibility_matrix.7.xml declares media.c2 as HIDL only;
+# an AIDL fragment makes checkUnusedHals fail the build. Converting is the right
+# long-term move and belongs with a target-level bump.
+#
+# THIS DOES NOT COST THE SOFTWARE CODECS. With hidl selected,
+# frameworks/av/media/module/codecserviceregistrant/CodecServiceRegistrant.cpp
+# registers the real platform store on the HIDL "software" instance and only a
+# null store on the AIDL one, and mediaserver's HIDL software manifest entry is
+# max-level 8, so it stays valid at target-level 7. Clients see two HIDL stores:
+# software (AOSP) and default (this one).
+#
+# Inside the gate on purpose: a build without the decoder keeps the aidl
+# cuttlefish sets.
+PRODUCT_VENDOR_PROPERTIES += \
+    media.c2.hal.selection=hidl
+
+# Codec2.0 poolMask. 0xf50000 selects ION for linear buffers, which is what
+# external/v4l2_codec2/README.md specifies for the ION case; BLOB (0xfc0000)
+# would be the alternative. Left at the README's ION value because that is what
+# the plugin store's allocator ids assume.
+PRODUCT_PROPERTY_OVERRIDES += \
+    debug.stagefright.c2-poolmask=0xf50000
+
+endif # XT_DOMA_HWDEC
+
 # Bluetooth off on a fresh userdata. A runtime overlay, not the one above: Car's own RRO
 # sets def_bluetooth_on=true and outranks any build-time overlay. The ordering that makes
 # this one win is in its Android.bp.
diff --git a/sepolicy/file_contexts b/sepolicy/file_contexts
index 3a6d344..91ff639 100644
--- a/sepolicy/file_contexts
+++ b/sepolicy/file_contexts
@@ -24,3 +24,17 @@
 # default already has the rules it needs for that type (22 references in the built
 # vendor_sepolicy.cil), so the apex hostapd works with no policy of its own.
 /data/vendor/wifi/shadow(/.*)?   u:object_r:hostapd_data_file:s0
+
+# The v4l2 Codec2 HAL. Without this line the binary stays vendor_file and init
+# cannot exec it at all (neverallow init ...:file execute_no_trans), so the
+# service never starts and the whole hardware-decode path is dead regardless of
+# whether a decoder is behind it. See sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te.
+#
+# The name carries the -64 suffix because soong appends it: the module is
+# android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2 and what lands in the image is
+# android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2-64. Matching without the suffix
+# labels nothing, which is the same silent failure as having no line here.
+#
+# Regex-escaped because '@' and '.' are literal here and file_contexts entries
+# are regexes.
+/vendor/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.media\.c2@1\.2-service-v4l2-64  u:object_r:hal_codec2_default_exec:s0
diff --git a/sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te b/sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7585417
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sepolicy/hal_codec2_default.te
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+#
+# Domain for the v4l2 Codec2 HAL, external/v4l2_codec2's
+# android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2-64. It is the DomA half of the
+# hardware-decode path: DomD owns bcm2835-codec, qemu's virtio-media device
+# proxies it, and this service is what Android's media framework talks to.
+#
+# WHY THIS FILE HAD TO BE WRITTEN AT ALL. Nothing in the tree labelled the
+# service binary, so it stayed vendor_file, and
+#
+#     system/sepolicy/private/init.te
+#       neverallow init { file_type fs_type }:file execute_no_trans;
+#
+# means init cannot exec an unlabelled binary without a domain to transition
+# into. The service therefore could not start -- in EITHER Xen config, with or
+# without a decoder behind it: the failure comes one step before a C2 HAL
+# could crash-loop for want of a /dev/videoN, and it has nothing to do with
+# the device.
+#
+# WHY IT IS NOT INSIDE THE XT_DOMA_HWDEC GATE, unlike the rest of the
+# hardware-decode wiring. A domain and an exec label with no binary to apply
+# them to are dead weight, not a hazard, whereas gating them would mean the
+# neverallow surface only gets exercised on hwdec builds -- and inventing a
+# domain is exactly where this product's sepolicy risk lives (see the note on
+# doma-wifi-relay in file_contexts). Keeping it
+# unconditional means every build type-checks the same policy.
+#
+# WHAT THE ATTRIBUTE ALREADY GIVES US, so this file stays short:
+# system/sepolicy/private/hal_codec2.te grants hal_codec2_server the binder
+# calls in both directions, hal_codec2_hwservice and hal_codec2_service access,
+# the media_variant and codec2_config properties, and the graphics/camera fd
+# use rules. hal_server_domain() below joins this domain to hal_codec2_server,
+# so none of that is repeated here.
+type hal_codec2_default, domain;
+hal_server_domain(hal_codec2_default, hal_codec2)
+
+type hal_codec2_default_exec, exec_type, vendor_file_type, file_type;
+init_daemon_domain(hal_codec2_default)
+
+# The decoder itself. On this board /dev/video* is NOT local hardware: it is
+# whatever qemu's virtio-media device exports, so the node exists only when
+# DomA's config has that device. video_device is the platform label for
+# /dev/video[0-9]* (system/sepolicy/private/file_contexts).
+#
+# Note that V4L2ComponentStore::Create() probes these nodes at service start to
+# decide which codecs to advertise, so a denial here does not look like a
+# permission error to the framework -- it looks like "no hardware decoder", and
+# the codec silently does not appear. If HW decode is missing on hardware, read
+# the avc log before anything else:
+#     logcat -d | grep -E 'avc: denied.*hal_codec2|haveDevice'
+allow hal_codec2_default video_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;
+
+# --- 2026-09-01: the three denials measured on the RPi4 board ---------------
+# All three were observed while the build is PERMISSIVE
+# (androidboot.selinux=permissive + enforcing=0 in
+# device/epam/aosp-xenvm-trout/xenvm_trout_arm64/BoardConfig.mk), which means
+# they did not break anything yet. They WILL break hardware decode the moment
+# the build goes enforcing, so they are allowed now while the evidence is fresh.
+# The denials quoted below are verbatim from a board log, 2026-09-01.
+
+# avc: denied { call } scontext=hal_codec2_default tcontext=hal_graphics_allocator_default tclass=binder
+#
+# The decoder allocates its own output buffers through gralloc. The platform
+# grants this binder call to the hal_graphics_allocator_CLIENT attribute
+# (system/sepolicy/private/hal_graphics_allocator.te), not to individual
+# domains, and hal_server_domain() above only joins hal_codec2_server. So the
+# fix is to join the client attribute -- writing a bare allow rule for
+# hal_graphics_allocator_default would work today and rot the moment the
+# platform renames the type.
+hal_client_domain(hal_codec2_default, hal_graphics_allocator)
+
+# avc: denied { ioctl } path="/dev/dri/renderD128" ioctlcmd=0x642e tcontext=gpu_device tclass=chr_file
+#
+# 0x642e is DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE: importing the gralloc dmabuf so it can
+# be described to the decoder. gpu_device has no ioctl allow-list anywhere in
+# system/sepolicy (no allowxperm/neverallowxperm on it), so a plain allow is
+# sufficient and no allowxperm is needed -- adding one would in fact be the
+# thing that starts restricting it.
+allow hal_codec2_default gpu_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;
+
+# avc: denied { getattr } path="/dev/dma_heap/system-uncached" tcontext=... tclass=chr_file
+#
+# dmabuf_system_heap_device is the label of the /dev/dma_heap/system* NODES
+# (system/sepolicy/private/file_contexts); dmabuf_heap_device labels the
+# DIRECTORY and is a different type -- and the directory is already granted to
+# every domain by public/domain.te, which is why only the chr_file rule is
+# missing. r_file_perms covers getattr. Same shape as the platform's own
+# mediacodec domain (system/sepolicy/vendor/mediacodec.te).
+allow hal_codec2_default dmabuf_system_heap_device:chr_file r_file_perms;
diff --git a/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/codec2.vendor.ext.policy b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/codec2.vendor.ext.policy
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0568c25
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/codec2.vendor.ext.policy
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+#
+# Extra syscalls the V4L2 codec2 service needs on top of
+# android.hardware.media.c2-default-seccomp_policy, per
+# external/v4l2_codec2/README.md.
+#
+# The README's list is written for 32-bit ABIs (mmap2, fstat64, _llseek...).
+# This product is arm64 only (TARGET_ARCH_VARIANT armv8-a, cortex-a72), so the
+# 64-bit names are used and the 32-bit-only entries are omitted -- listing a
+# syscall that does not exist on the ABI makes minijail reject the whole policy.
+epoll_create1: 1
+epoll_ctl: 1
+epoll_pwait: 1
+eventfd2: 1
+getcwd: 1
+getdents64: 1
+pselect6: 1
+statfs: 1
+sysinfo: 1
diff --git a/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7e33ca0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xenvm_trout_rpi4_arm64/media_codecs_c2.xml
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+
+     Codec2 components provided by external/v4l2_codec2, for the DomA
+     hardware-decode path.
+
+     DECODERS ONLY, AND H.264 ONLY.
+
+     The hardware behind this is VideoCore's decoder, reached through
+     virtio-media and bcm2835-codec in DomD. bcm2835-v4l2-codec.c supports
+     H.263, H.264, JPEG, MJPEG, MPEG2, MPEG4 and VC-1 (no VP8, VP9, HEVC or
+     AV1), so
+     the VP8/VP9 entries in external/v4l2_codec2/README.md's template would
+     advertise codecs that cannot work. Of the formats it does support, H.264 is
+     the one this product's decode path was built and measured on, so avc is the
+     only entry that earns its place.
+
+     The encoder entries are dropped for the same reason plus one more:
+     bcm2835-codec's encode function is a different node (/dev/video11) and this
+     device model proxies exactly one node.
+
+     The .secure variants are dropped: they need Widevine and a secure
+     allocator, neither of which exists here.
+
+     max size 1920x1088 rather than 4096x4096: VideoCore's H.264 decoder is a
+     1080p part. Claiming 4K would let the framework hand it streams it cannot
+     decode, and the failure would look like a decoder bug.
+
+     THE TWO RATE LIMITS SAY DIFFERENT THINGS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE (2026-08-30).
+     1920x1080 is divUp(1920,16) x divUp(1080,16) = 120 x 68 = 8160 macroblocks, so
+
+         blocks-per-second max 489600  = 8160 x 60  = 1080p60
+         performance-point-1920x1080 30-30           = 1080p30
+
+     blocks-per-second is the HARDWARE's rating: VideoCore decodes 1080p60 H.264.
+     The performance point is what THIS PATH is expected to sustain, and this path
+     bounces every frame twice between the guest SGL and a host MMAP buffer because
+     bcm2835-codec does not accept USERPTR (about 190 MB/s round trip at 1080p30
+     NV12). 30 is a conservative placeholder, NOT a measurement.
+
+     NEITHER NUMBER REACHES WEBVIEW. Measured on the shipped WebView
+     (145.0.7632.218, product/app/webview/webview.apk): its dex references
+     getVideoCapabilities, getSupportedWidths, isSizeSupported and
+     isFeatureSupported, but NOT areSizeAndRateSupported and NOT
+     getSupportedPerformancePoints, and neither name appears in
+     libwebviewchromium.so either. Chromium therefore selects on size and features
+     alone. Do not expect either limit to stop a page playing video in a WebView
+     from asking for 1080p60; if that has to be capped, cap it in the app that
+     embeds the WebView.
+
+     performance-point is a pure information API in any case: nothing in
+     frameworks/base/media or frameworks/av consults
+     getSupportedPerformancePoints() during codec selection (all 8 references are
+     getters, JNI read-out or the interface declaration).
+
+     NOTE for whoever edits this file: an XML comment may not contain two
+     consecutive hyphens. It is worse than a build break here:
+     MediaCodecsXmlParser reports BAD_VALUE
+     for a malformed file, combineStatus() does NOT downgrade that to OK, and
+     Codec2InfoBuilder.cpp:503-506 then returns without registering ANY Codec2
+     component. A stray double hyphen makes the hardware decoder vanish from
+     MediaCodecList entirely.
+-->
+<MediaCodecs>
+   <Decoders>
+       <MediaCodec name="c2.v4l2.avc.decoder" type="video/avc" >
+           <Limit name="size" min="16x16" max="1920x1088" />
+           <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
+           <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
+           <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="489600" />
+           <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-62500000" />
+           <Limit name="concurrent-instances" max="8" />
+           <Limit name="performance-point-1920x1080" range="30-30" />
+           <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
+       </MediaCodec>
+   </Decoders>
+</MediaCodecs>
